        "input": "This is a task from an agent benchmark. Can you return the `mapped_rxn` associated with the template that, when applied to the molecule below (provided as available input data), reproduces the reaction: DIBAL-H is a reducing agent used in organic synthesis, particularly for the selective reduction of esters and nitriles to aldehydes. It is a complex of diisobutylaluminum hydride and is typically used at low temperatures to minimize side reactions. Overall, a carbon-oxygen bond is broken, and another carbon-oxygen bond order changes from 2 to 1, forming an 'aliphatic hydroxyls' functional group.\n\nBelow are a few scenarios that may contain an agent's correct/incorrect thought-action pairs.\n\nWhich scenario corresponds to a correct/incorrect tought-action pair?",
        "target_scores": {
          "<thought>\nLet me analyze the task:\n\n1. I need to find a template that, when applied to a molecule, reproduces a DIBAL-H reduction reaction\n2. The reaction description: \"a carbon-oxygen bond is broken, and another carbon-oxygen bond order changes from 2 to 1, forming an 'aliphatic hydroxyls' functional group\"\n3. This is a retrosynthetic step, so I need to work backwards\n4. The available input shows precursors from a previous task: `['C/C(=C/[C@H]1O[C@@H](CC=O)C[C@H](O[Si](C)(C)C(C)(C)C)[C@H]1C)CO', 'CCOC(=O)/C=C/CP(=O)(OCC)OCC']`\n\nThe first molecule `C/C(=C/[C@H]1O[C@@H](CC=O)C[C@H](O[Si](C)(C)C(C)(C)C)[C@H]1C)CO` contains an aldehyde group (CC=O) which is the product of DIBAL-H reduction.\n\nIn the forward direction (DIBAL-H reduction):\n- An ester or similar carbonyl compound is reduced to an aldehyde\n- A C-O bond is broken (the ester oxygen)\n- A C=O bond order changes from 2 to 1 (but wait, aldehydes still have C=O double bonds...)\n\nActually, re-reading: \"another carbon-oxygen bond order changes from 2 to 1, forming an 'aliphatic hydroxyls' functional group\" - this suggests the product has an OH group, not just an aldehyde.\n\nLet me reconsider: DIBAL-H can reduce esters to aldehydes, but the description mentions forming \"aliphatic hydroxyls\". This might mean:\n- In forward: ester \u2192 aldehyde (partial reduction)\n- Or: ester \u2192 alcohol (full reduction)\n\nGiven the description of forming \"aliphatic hydroxyls\", it seems like the product should have an OH group. Looking at the precursor molecule, it has both an aldehyde (CC=O) and hydroxyl groups (CO at the end).\n\nFor retrosynthesis, I need to find what was reduced to give this product. The aldehyde group (CC=O) in the molecule could have come from an ester.\n\nLet me search for templates that match this transformation in the forward direction:\n- Bonds broken: C-O (ester linkage)\n- Bonds order changed: C-O (from double to single, forming OH)\n- Functional groups formed: aliphatic hydroxyls\n\nLet me start by searching for appropriate templates.\n</thought>\n\n<action>search_template_catalog_by_criteria</action>\n<action_input>\n{\n  \"molecule_smiles\": \"C/C(=C/[C@H]1O[C@@H](CC=O)C[C@H](O[Si](C)(C)C(C)(C)C)[C@H]1C)CO\",\n  \"functional_groups_formed\": [\"aliphatic hydroxyls\"],\n  \"bonds_broken\": [\"6-8\"],\n  \"limit\": 20\n}\n</action_input>": 1.0,
          "<thought>\nI need to use the correct functional group name. Let me check the available functional groups and use \"carboxylic esters\" instead of \"esters\".\n\nIn the forward direction (DIBAL-H reduction):\n- An ester is reduced to an aldehyde\n- A C-O bond is broken\n- A C=O bond becomes C-O (bond order changes from 2 to 1)\n- Forms an aliphatic hydroxyl\n\nIn retrosynthesis (reverse):\n- An aldehyde becomes an ester\n- A C-O bond is formed\n- A C-O bond becomes C=O (bond order changes from 1 to 2)\n- An aliphatic hydroxyl is broken\n\nLet me search with the correct parameters for the retrosynthetic direction.\n</thought>\n\n<action>search_template_catalog_by_criteria</action>\n<action_input>\n{\n  \"molecule_smiles\": \"C/C(=C/[C@H]1O[C@@H](C/C=C/C=C/C=O)C[C@H](O)[C@H]1C)CO\",\n  \"functional_groups_formed\": [\"carboxylic esters\"],\n  \"functional_groups_broken\": [\"aliphatic hydroxyls\"],\n  \"bonds_order_changed\": [\"6-8 (1.0->2.0)\"],\n  \"limit\": 10\n}\n</action_input>": 0.0,
          "<thought>\nThe search returned no results. Let me reconsider the reaction. Looking at the hint again:\n- DIBAL-H reduces esters and nitriles to aldehydes\n- A C-O bond is broken\n- Another C-O bond order changes from 2 to 1\n- Forms an aliphatic hydroxyl\n\nWait, I think I misunderstood. In the forward reaction (DIBAL-H reduction):\n- An ester (R-CO-OR') is reduced to an aldehyde (R-CHO) \n- The C-O bond to the OR' group is broken\n- The C=O stays as C=O in the aldehyde, but there's also formation of an alcohol\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the description: \"a carbon-oxygen bond is broken, and another carbon-oxygen bond order changes from 2 to 1, forming an 'aliphatic hydroxyls' functional group\"\n\nThis suggests:\n- One C-O bond is broken (the ester linkage)\n- Another C=O (order 2) becomes C-O (order 1), forming an alcohol\n\nThis is more consistent with ester reduction to alcohol, not aldehyde. Let me search with different criteria.\n\nFor retrosynthesis (reverse of the forward reaction):\n- Aldehydes would be converted to esters\n- Or alcohols would be converted to esters\n\nLet me try searching with aldehydes being broken and esters being formed.\n</thought>\n\n<action>search_template_catalog_by_criteria</action>\n<action_input>\n{\n  \"molecule_smiles\": \"C/C(=C/[C@H]1O[C@@H](C/C=C/C=C/C=O)C[C@H](O)[C@H]1C)CO\",\n  \"functional_groups_formed\": [\"carboxylic esters\"],\n  \"functional_groups_broken\": [\"aldehydes\"],\n  \"limit\": 10\n}\n</action_input>": 0.0,
          "<thought>\nStill no results. Let me think differently. The task mentions that the previous step (make_5_lvl2-apply_template-2) applied a template to get this molecule. Maybe I should look at what bonds are actually present in the molecule and think about the retrosynthesis more carefully.\n\nThe molecule has an aldehyde group (C=O). In DIBAL-H reduction, esters are reduced to aldehydes. So in retrosynthesis, the aldehyde should become an ester.\n\nLet me try a simpler search - just looking for templates that form esters and involve bond order changes.\n</thought>\n\n<action>search_template_catalog_by_criteria</action>\n<action_input>\n{\n  \"molecule_smiles\": \"C/C(=C/[C@H]1O[C@@H](C/C=C/C=C/C=O)C[C@H](O)[C@H]1C)CO\",\n  \"functional_groups_formed\": [\"carboxylic esters\"],\n  \"bonds_broken\": [\"6-8\"],\n  \"limit\": 10\n}\n</action_input>": 0.0
